---
import BaseLayout from '@/layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleContent from '@/components/articles/ArticleContent.astro';
import ArticleFAQ from '@/components/articles/ArticleFAQ.astro';
import StatsBar from '@/components/articles/StatsBar.astro';
import ArticleContactForm from '@/components/articles/ArticleContactForm.astro';
import ArticleTestimonials from '@/components/articles/ArticleTestimonials.astro';

const faqItems = [
  { question: "What are economic damages in a personal injury case?", answer: "Economic damages are the quantifiable financial losses you suffer as a result of your injuries. These include current and future medical expenses, lost wages, diminished earning capacity, property damage, and out-of-pocket expenses related to your injury. They are typically calculated based on actual bills, receipts, and expert projections." },
  { question: "What are non-economic damages?", answer: "Non-economic damages compensate you for non-financial losses that are harder to quantify, such as pain and suffering, emotional distress, loss of enjoyment of life, loss of companionship, mental anguish, and disfigurement. While these damages don't have a direct dollar value, they reflect the real impact your injuries have on your quality of life." },
  { question: "Can I recover punitive damages in Michigan?", answer: "Yes, Michigan law allows for punitive damages in cases involving egregious misconduct. Punitive damages are meant to punish the wrongdoer and deter similar conduct in the future, rather than to compensate the victim. They are typically awarded in cases involving gross negligence, malice, or intentional wrongdoing." },
  { question: "How is the value of my personal injury case determined?", answer: "The value of your case depends on factors including the severity of your injuries, the cost of medical treatment, the length of your recovery, the impact on your earning capacity, the strength of the evidence showing the other party's liability, available insurance coverage, and the jurisdiction where your case is filed. An experienced attorney can evaluate these factors to estimate your case's worth." },
  { question: "What is a damage cap in Michigan personal injury cases?", answer: "Michigan has historically had damage caps on certain types of cases, particularly medical malpractice. However, there are generally no caps on economic damages in standard personal injury cases. Non-economic damages may be subject to certain limitations depending on the type of case. Our attorneys can explain how caps may apply to your specific situation." },
  { question: "How long do I have to seek compensation for my injuries?", answer: "Michigan's statute of limitations generally gives you three years from the date of injury to file a personal injury lawsuit. However, there are exceptions that can shorten or extend this deadline, such as for injuries to minors or claims against government entities. Contact an attorney promptly to ensure you don't miss important deadlines." },
];
---
<BaseLayout title="Types of Damages Recoverable in Personal Injury Lawsuits | Ann Arbor MI" description="Learn about the different types of damages recoverable in Michigan personal injury lawsuits, including economic, non-economic, and punitive damages. Our experienced attorneys explain how case values are determined.">
  <ArticleContent>
    <p>If you've been injured due to someone else's negligence in Michigan, you may be entitled to several different types of compensation, commonly referred to as "damages." Understanding the categories of damages available in <a href="/personal-injury-overview/">personal injury lawsuits</a> can help you and your attorney evaluate the true value of your claim and pursue the maximum compensation you deserve.</p>
    <h2><strong>Economic Damages</strong></h2>
    <p>Economic damages, also called special damages, are intended to compensate you for the quantifiable financial losses you have suffered or will suffer as a result of your injuries. These damages are typically straightforward to calculate because they are based on actual expenses and verifiable losses.</p>
    <p><strong>Common types of economic damages include:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Medical expenses:</strong> Current and future medical bills, including hospital stays, surgeries, doctor visits, prescription medications, physical therapy, and rehabilitation.</li>
      <li><strong>Lost wages:</strong> Income you have lost while recovering from your injuries, as well as any bonuses, commissions, or benefits you missed out on.</li>
      <li><strong>Loss of earning capacity:</strong> Compensation for reduced ability to earn income in the future if your injuries prevent you from returning to your previous job or working at full capacity.</li>
      <li><strong>Property damage:</strong> The cost to repair or replace damaged property, such as your vehicle in a <a href="/practice-area/car-accidents/">car accident</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Out-of-pocket expenses:</strong> Any costs you incurred related to your injury, such as transportation to medical appointments, home modifications, or household services you couldn't perform.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2><strong>Non-Economic Damages</strong></h2>
    <p>Non-economic damages, also called general damages, compensate you for non-financial losses that are more subjective in nature. While these damages are harder to quantify, they often represent a significant portion of a personal injury award because they reflect the real human impact of the injury.</p>
    <p><strong>Common types of non-economic damages include:</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Pain and suffering:</strong> Compensation for the physical pain and discomfort caused by your injuries, both during treatment and any ongoing or permanent pain.</li>
      <li><strong>Emotional distress:</strong> Damages for psychological impacts such as anxiety, depression, PTSD, and fear.</li>
      <li><strong>Loss of enjoyment of life:</strong> Compensation for the inability to participate in hobbies, activities, and daily pleasures you enjoyed before the injury.</li>
      <li><strong>Loss of consortium:</strong> Damages awarded to family members for the loss of companionship, affection, and intimate relations.</li>
      <li><strong>Disfigurement and scarring:</strong> Compensation for permanent physical changes that affect your appearance and self-esteem.</li>
      <li><strong>Mental anguish:</strong> Damages for severe emotional suffering.</li>
    </ul>
    <h2><strong>Punitive Damages</strong></h2>
    <p>Punitive damages are awarded in addition to compensatory damages (both economic and non-economic) and are designed to punish the wrongdoer for particularly reckless or malicious conduct and to deter similar behavior in the future. In Michigan, punitive damages may be awarded when the defendant acted with gross negligence, malice, or willful and wanton misconduct.</p>
    <p>Examples of cases that may warrant punitive damages include drunk driving accidents, fraud, intentional harm, or cases involving extreme disregard for human safety. While punitive damages are not available in every case, they can substantially increase the total recovery when they are awarded.</p>
    <h2><strong>Factors That Affect the Value of Your Damages</strong></h2>
    <p>Several factors influence the amount of damages you may be entitled to recover:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>The severity and permanence of your injuries</li>
      <li>The strength of the evidence proving the other party's liability</li>
      <li>The cost of your medical treatment and future care needs</li>
      <li>The impact of your injuries on your ability to work and enjoy life</li>
      <li>Available insurance coverage</li>
      <li>The jurisdiction where your case is filed</li>
    </ul>
    <h2><strong>Michigan's Comparative Negligence Rule</strong></h2>
    <p>Michigan follows a comparative negligence rule, which means your damages may be reduced by the percentage of fault attributed to you. If you are found to be more than 50% at fault, you may be barred from recovering any damages. Insurance companies often try to increase your percentage of fault to minimize the amount they have to pay.</p>
    <h2><strong>Contact Our Experienced Personal Injury Attorneys</strong></h2>
    <p>If you've been injured due to someone else's negligence, understanding the types of damages available in your case is critical to pursuing fair compensation. At <a href="/about-us/">Logeman, Iafrate & Logeman, P.C.</a>, our experienced Michigan personal injury attorneys have helped countless clients recover full and fair compensation for their injuries for over 45 years.</p>
    <p>We offer free consultations to evaluate your case and explain your legal options. Contact us today to schedule a no-obligation case review.</p>
  </ArticleContent>

  <ArticleFAQ items={faqItems} />

  <StatsBar />

  <ArticleContactForm />

  <ArticleTestimonials />
</BaseLayout>
